WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.335
Orchard Core exports its traces, metrics and logs with OpenTelemetry, to any backend that receives OTLP.

00:00:07.335 --> 00:00:11.596
Each of them tells which tenant it comes from, and which instance of

00:00:11.596 --> 00:00:13.554
the application produced it.

00:00:13.554 --> 00:00:17.643
In this video, we'll enable it, look at what it collects, and see

00:00:17.643 --> 00:00:19.140
how to configure it.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:30.248
An Orchard Core application often serves many tenants, from several instances behind a load balancer.

00:00:30.248 --> 00:00:34.688
When a page is slow, or an error shows up, the first questions are:

00:00:34.688 --> 00:00:37.060
which tenant, and which instance?

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:44.824
OpenTelemetry is the open standard for traces, metrics and logs, and OTLP is its protocol.

00:00:44.824 --> 00:00:52.350
Grafana, Jaeger, Azure Monitor, Datadog, Honeycomb, and the .NET Aspire dashboard all receive it.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.432
Orchard Core tags every span, log entry and request metric with its tenant,

00:00:58.432 --> 00:01:02.315
and describes each instance with resource attributes.

00:01:02.315 --> 00:01:04.553
It also traces its own work:

00:01:04.553 --> 00:01:10.280
building and activating tenants, running background tasks, and executing recipes.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.954
To enable it, call Add Open Telemetry on the Orchard Core builder, in

00:01:18.954 --> 00:01:21.459
the Program file of the application.

00:01:21.459 --> 00:01:24.800
The Orchard Core CMS web application already does.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:30.696
Then, set the address of an OTLP receiver, with the standard OTLP endpoint

00:01:30.696 --> 00:01:35.905
environment variable, or in the Open Telemetry section of the app settings.

00:01:35.905 --> 00:01:39.250
Until an endpoint is set, nothing is exported.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.374
To try it locally, the .NET Aspire dashboard is a free OTLP receiver

00:01:44.374 --> 00:01:45.911
that runs in Docker.

00:01:45.911 --> 00:01:50.758
Here, two instances of the application, each serving a Default and an Agency

00:01:50.758 --> 00:01:53.300
tenant, export their telemetry to it.

00:01:57.469 --> 00:02:02.945
In the Traces page, each request is a trace, from both instances of the application.

00:02:02.945 --> 00:02:08.361
Filtered on the Blog recipe, only the traces that set up the Default tenant remain.

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:09.639
Let's open one.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.517
Its spans show what Orchard Core did:

00:02:12.517 --> 00:02:17.991
building the service container of the tenant, activating it, building its request pipeline,

00:02:17.991 --> 00:02:21.660
and executing the Blog recipe, with one span for each step.

00:02:22.113 --> 00:02:26.787
Each span carries the name of its tenant, and here, of its recipe.

00:02:26.787 --> 00:02:30.033
The slowest step of a setup is easy to find.

00:02:30.486 --> 00:02:34.508
The resource attributes identify the instance that produced it:

00:02:34.508 --> 00:02:39.054
the service instance ID, the host name, the process, and the versions of

00:02:39.054 --> 00:02:40.686
.NET and Orchard Core.

00:02:44.876 --> 00:02:49.394
In the Metrics page, the request duration of ASP.NET Core has a dimension

00:02:49.394 --> 00:02:54.426
for the Orchard Core tenant, so the response times of each tenant can be compared.

00:02:54.448 --> 00:02:59.510
The Orchard Core meter counts the tenants by state, and the tenants loaded in memory.

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:03.737
It also measures how long each tenant takes to build, to activate, and

00:03:03.737 --> 00:03:05.628
to run its background tasks.

00:03:06.079 --> 00:03:11.281
With the .NET runtime metrics, such as memory, garbage collections and the thread

00:03:11.281 --> 00:03:14.330
pool, the health of each instance is visible.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.139
Each instance can be selected on its own.

00:03:21.327 --> 00:03:23.838
Structured logs carry their tenant too.

00:03:23.838 --> 00:03:27.857
Filtered on the Agency tenant, only what happened in it is listed.

00:03:28.311 --> 00:03:33.309
Each entry keeps its message, its parameters, the trace it belongs to, and

00:03:33.309 --> 00:03:35.371
the instance that wrote it.

00:03:39.571 --> 00:03:44.594
The Open Telemetry section turns each signal on or off, samples traces, adds

00:03:44.594 --> 00:03:50.781
sources and meters, and can remove the tenant from request metrics when there are many tenants.

00:03:51.531 --> 00:03:57.092
An application that already configures OpenTelemetry, such as a .NET Aspire solution with

00:03:57.092 --> 00:04:03.531
its service defaults, adds the Orchard Core instrumentation to its tracing, metrics and logging instead.

00:04:04.281 --> 00:04:09.305
Modules can trace their own work with the Orchard Core activity source and meter.

00:04:09.305 --> 00:04:14.791
They only depend on System dot Diagnostics, so the .NET counters tool can watch them too.

00:04:16.791 --> 00:04:22.294
OpenTelemetry shows what every tenant and every instance of Orchard Core is doing,

00:04:22.294 --> 00:04:24.545
in the backend of your choice.

00:04:24.545 --> 00:04:26.171
Thanks for watching.

